```java
public class GamePauseManager {
    private boolean gamePaused;
    private List<Timer> timers;
    private List<GraphicsRenderingCallback> renderingCallbacks;

    public GamePauseManager() {
        this.gamePaused = false;
        this.timers = new ArrayList<>();
        this.renderingCallbacks = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    public void pauseGame() {
        if (!gamePaused) {
            gamePaused = true;
            // Pause ongoing tasks
            for (Timer timer : timers) {
                timer.stop();
            }
            // Disable timers
            for (GraphicsRenderingCallback callback : renderingCallbacks) {
                callback.disableRendering();
            }
            // Invalidate graphics rendering callbacks
        }
    }

    public void resumeGame() {
        if (gamePaused) {
            gamePaused = false;
            // Resume ongoing tasks
            for (Timer timer : timers) {
                timer.start();
            }
            // Enable timers
            for (GraphicsRenderingCallback callback : renderingCallbacks) {
                callback.enableRendering();
            }
            // Revalidate graphics rendering callbacks
        }
    }

    // Additional methods to manage timers and rendering callbacks
    public void addTimer(Timer timer) {
        timers.add(timer);
    }

    public void addRenderingCallback(GraphicsRenderingCallback callback) {
        renderingCallbacks.add(callback);
    }
}
```

In the solution, the `GamePauseManager` class maintains a boolean flag `gamePaused` to track the game's pause state. The `pauseGame` method stops ongoing tasks and disables timers and rendering callbacks when the game is paused. The `resumeGame` method resumes the tasks, enables timers, and revalidates rendering callbacks when the game is resumed. Additionally, the class provides methods to add timers and rendering callbacks to be managed during pause and resume operations.

