```rust
impl Map {
    fn enter(&self) -> Vec<Vec<Cave>> {
        let mut paths = Vec::new();
        if let Some(start) = self.caves.get("start") {
            self.explore(&start, vec![start.clone()], &mut paths);
        }
        paths
    }

    fn explore(&self, cave: &Cave, mut current_path: Vec<Cave>, paths: &mut Vec<Vec<Cave>>) {
        for connected_cave_name in &cave.connected_caves {
            if let Some(connected_cave) = self.caves.get(connected_cave_name) {
                if !current_path.iter().any(|c| c.name == connected_cave.name) {
                    current_path.push(connected_cave.clone());
                    if connected_cave.connected_caves.is_empty() {
                        paths.push(current_path.clone());
                    } else {
                        self.explore(connected_cave, current_path.clone(), paths);
                    }
                    current_path.pop();
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In the solution, the `enter` method initializes an empty vector `paths` to store the possible paths and then starts the exploration from the "start" cave by calling the `explore` method. The `explore` method recursively explores the connected caves and adds the paths to the `paths` vector when reaching the end of a path. The algorithm uses depth-first search to explore all possible paths.

