def longestIncreasingSubsequence(nums):
    n = len(nums)
    if n == 0:
        return 0

    dp = [1] * n  # Initialize the dp array with 1 as the minimum LIS length for each element

    for i in range(1, n):
        for j in range(i):
            if nums[i] > nums[j]:
                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)  # Update the LIS length for element i

    return max(dp)  # Return the maximum LIS length found in the dp array
```

The `longestIncreasingSubsequence` function initializes a dynamic programming (dp) array with a length equal to the input array. It then iterates through the input array, updating the dp array to store the length of the longest increasing subsequence ending at each index. Finally, it returns the maximum value in the dp array, which represents the length of the longest increasing subsequence in the input array.

