You are tasked with implementing a function to sort an array using the Quick Sort algorithm. Quick Sort is a comparison-based sorting algorithm that uses a divide-and-conquer strategy to sort elements efficiently. The algorithm works by selecting a 'pivot' element from the array and partitioning the other elements into two sub-arrays according to whether they are less than or greater than the pivot. The sub-arrays are then recursively sorted.

You are given a code snippet that demonstrates the recursive partitioning and sorting step of the Quick Sort algorithm. The `PartitionAndSort` function takes an array as input, partitions it into three sub-arrays (left, equal, and right), and then recursively sorts the left and right sub-arrays. The sorted sub-arrays are then concatenated together to form the sorted array.

Your task is to complete the implementation of the `PartitionAndSort` function and write the complete Quick Sort algorithm to sort the given array.

Code snippet for inspiration:
```csharp
public class QuickSort
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        int[] arr = { 5, 3, 8, 6, 2, 7, 1, 4 };
        PartitionAndSort(arr);
    }

    public static void PartitionAndSort(int[] arr)
    {
        if (arr.Length < 2)
        {
            return;
        }

        int pivot = arr[0];
        List<int> left = new List<int>();
        List<int> equal = new List<int>();
        List<int> right = new List<int>();

        foreach (int num in arr)
        {
            if (num < pivot)
            {
                left.Add(num);
            }
            else if (num > pivot)
            {
                right.Add(num);
            }
            else
            {
                equal.Add(num);
            }
        }

        List<int> output = new List<int>();
        output.AddRange(PartitionAndSort(left.ToArray()));
        output.AddRange(equal);
        output.AddRange(PartitionAndSort(right.ToArray()));
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(" ", output));
    }
}
```

output:
```csharp
public class QuickSort
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        int[] arr = { 5, 3, 8, 6, 2, 7, 1, 4 };
        QuickSortArray(arr, 0, arr.Length - 1);
        Console.WriteLine("Sorted Array: " + string.Join(" ", arr));
    }

    public static void QuickSortArray(int[] arr, int low, int high)
    {
        if (low < high)
        {
            int pivotIndex = Partition(arr, low, high);
            QuickSortArray(arr, low, pivotIndex - 1);
            QuickSortArray(arr, pivotIndex + 1, high);
        }
    }

    public static int Partition(int[] arr, int low, int high)
    {
        int pivot = arr[high];
        int i = low - 1;

        for (int j = low; j < high; j++)
        {
            if (arr[j] < pivot)
            {
                i++;
                Swap(arr, i, j);
            }
        }

        Swap(arr, i + 1, high);
        return i + 1;
    }

    public static void Swap(int[] arr, int i, int j)
    {
        int temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[j];
        arr[j] = temp;
    }
}
```

The `QuickSortArray` function is the entry point for the Quick Sort algorithm. It takes the input array, along with the low and high indices, and recursively sorts the array using the `Partition` function. The `Partition` function selects the pivot element and rearranges the elements in the array such that all elements less than the pivot are on the left and all elements greater than the pivot are on the right. The `Swap` function is used to swap elements in the array. Finally, the sorted array is printed in the `Main` method.

