- `value`: an integer representing the value of the token
- `priority`: an integer representing the priority of the token

The custom sorting algorithm should sort a vector of `Token` objects based on the following rules:
1. Tokens with higher priority should come before tokens with lower priority.
2. If two tokens have the same priority, the one with the higher value should come before the one with the lower value.

You need to implement the sorting algorithm and apply it to a given vector of `Token` objects.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>

class Token {
public:
    int value;
    int priority;

    Token(int v, int p) : value(v), priority(p) {}
};

// Custom sorting algorithm
void customSort(std::vector<Token>& tokens) {
    std::sort(tokens.begin(), tokens.end(), [](const Token& t1, const Token& t2) {
        if (t1.priority != t2.priority) {
            return t1.priority > t2.priority;
        } else {
            return t1.value > t2.value;
        }
    });
}

int main() {
    std::vector<Token> tokens = {Token(3, 2), Token(5, 1), Token(4, 3), Token(2, 2)};
    customSort(tokens);

    for (const Token& token : tokens) {
        std::cout << "Value: " << token.value << ", Priority: " << token.priority << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

