from collections import Counter

class Solution(object):
    def largestOverlap(self, A, B):
        n = len(A)
        count = Counter()
        max_overlap = 0

        for i in range(n):
            for j in range(n):
                if A[i][j] == 1:
                    for x in range(n):
                        for y in range(n):
                            if B[x][y] == 1:
                                count[(i - x, j - y)] += 1
                                max_overlap = max(max_overlap, count[(i - x, j - y)])

        return max_overlap
```
The solution uses a Counter to keep track of the number of overlaps for each relative position of A and B. It iterates through each cell of A and B, and for each pair of 1s, it calculates the relative position and increments the count in the Counter. Finally, it returns the maximum overlap count found. This solution has a time complexity of O(n^4) where n is the size of the matrices, and it efficiently handles large input matrices.

