The `isSameTree` method first checks if both `p` and `q` are `null`, in which case it returns `true` as they are structurally identical. If only one of `p` or `q` is `null`, it returns `false` as they are not structurally identical. Then, it compares the values of the current nodes and recursively checks the left and right subtrees for structural identity. If any of these checks fail, it returns `false`. If all checks pass, it returns `true`, indicating that the trees are structurally identical.

