import numpy as np

class Reorthogonalization:
    def __init__(self, n):
        self.n = n
        self.q = np.zeros((n, n))  # Initialize the set of vectors
        self.r = np.zeros((n, n))  # Initialize the matrix to store the reorthogonalization results

    def reorthomgs2(self):
        for j in np.arange(self.n):
            t = np.linalg.norm(self.q[j])  # Compute the norm of the vector self.q[j]
            nach = 1
            u = 0
            while nach:
                u += 1
                for i in np.arange(j):
                    s = np.dot(np.transpose(self.q[i]), self.q[j])  # Compute the dot product
                    self.r[i, j] = self.r[i, j] + s  # Update the reorthogonalization matrix
                    self.q[j] = self.q[j] - s * self.q[i]  # Update the vector self.q[j]
                if u >= 10 or np.linalg.norm(self.q[j]) >= 0.1 * t:
                    nach = 0  # Exit the reorthogonalization loop if conditions are met
```

In the solution, the `reorthomgs2` method is completed to perform the reorthogonalization process using the modified Gram-Schmidt method. The method iterates through each vector, updates the reorthogonalization matrix `self.r`, and modifies the vector `self.q[j]` to ensure orthogonality with respect to the previously processed vectors. The loop terminates when the specified conditions for reorthogonalization are met.

