```java
public interface Visitor {
    void visitFieldRef(FieldRef fieldRef);
}

public class FieldRef {
    private String fieldName;

    public FieldRef(String fieldName) {
        this.fieldName = fieldName;
    }

    public String getFieldName() {
        return fieldName;
    }

    public void accept(Visitor visitor) {
        visitor.visitFieldRef(this);
    }
}

public class FieldRefVisitor implements Visitor {
    @Override
    public void visitFieldRef(FieldRef fieldRef) {
        System.out.println("Visiting field reference: " + fieldRef.getFieldName());
    }
}

public class Client {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FieldRef fieldRef = new FieldRef("exampleField");
        FieldRefVisitor fieldRefVisitor = new FieldRefVisitor();
        fieldRef.accept(fieldRefVisitor);
    }
}
```

When the `Client` class is executed, it will create a `FieldRef` object with the field name "exampleField" and a `FieldRefVisitor` object. Then, it will call the `accept` method of the `FieldRef` object, passing the `FieldRefVisitor` as an argument. This will trigger the visit to the `FieldRef` object by the `FieldRefVisitor`, resulting in the message "Visiting field reference: exampleField" being printed to the console.

