```rust
fn movePlayer(grid: &mut [[i32; 5]; 5], direction: &str) -> (usize, usize) {
    let mut player_position = (0, 0);
    for (i, row) in grid.iter().enumerate() {
        for (j, &cell) in row.iter().enumerate() {
            if cell == 1 {
                player_position = (i, j);
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    let (mut x, mut y) = player_position;
    match direction {
        "up" => {
            if x > 0 {
                x -= 1;
            }
        }
        "down" => {
            if x < grid.len() - 1 {
                x += 1;
            }
        }
        "left" => {
            if y > 0 {
                y -= 1;
            }
        }
        "right" => {
            if y < grid[0].len() - 1 {
                y += 1;
            }
        }
        _ => {}
    }

    grid[player_position.0][player_position.1] = 0; // Clear the previous position
    grid[x][y] = 1; // Update the new position

    (x, y)
}
```

This solution defines a function `movePlayer` that iterates through the grid to find the current position of the player. It then updates the position based on the given direction, ensuring that the player stays within the boundaries of the grid. Finally, it updates the grid with the new player position and returns the updated position as a tuple.

