You are tasked with implementing a custom stack data structure in C#. The stack should support the following operations:
1. Push: Add an element to the top of the stack.
2. Pop: Remove and return the element at the top of the stack.
3. Peek: Return the element at the top of the stack without removing it.
4. IsEmpty: Return true if the stack is empty, false otherwise.

Your implementation should use the provided `System.Collections` namespace for reference but should not directly use the built-in `Stack` class. You should create a class called `CustomStack` with the necessary methods to achieve the functionality described above.

```csharp
using System;

public class CustomStack<T>
{
    private T[] elements;
    private int top;

    public CustomStack(int capacity)
    {
        elements = new T[capacity];
        top = -1;
    }

    public void Push(T item)
    {
        if (top == elements.Length - 1)
        {
            Array.Resize(ref elements, elements.Length * 2);
        }
        elements[++top] = item;
    }

    public T Pop()
    {
        if (top == -1)
        {
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return elements[top--];
    }

    public T Peek()
    {
        if (top == -1)
        {
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return elements[top];
    }

    public bool IsEmpty()
    {
        return top == -1;
    }
}
```

output:
```csharp
// Test the CustomStack implementation
public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        CustomStack<int> stack = new CustomStack<int>(5);
        stack.Push(10);
        stack.Push(20);
        stack.Push(30);

        Console.WriteLine(stack.Pop());  // Output: 30
        Console.WriteLine(stack.Peek()); // Output: 20
        Console.WriteLine(stack.IsEmpty()); // Output: false

        stack.Pop();
        stack.Pop();

        Console.WriteLine(stack.IsEmpty()); // Output: true
    }
}
```

The `CustomStack` class provides a custom implementation of a stack data structure using an array. The `Push` method adds an element to the top of the stack, resizing the array if necessary. The `Pop` method removes and returns the element at the top of the stack. The `Peek` method returns the element at the top of the stack without removing it. The `IsEmpty` method checks if the stack is empty. The `Main` method demonstrates the usage of the `CustomStack` class by performing various stack operations.

