import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class CoroutineManager {
    private List<Thread> runningCoroutines;

    public CoroutineManager() {
        runningCoroutines = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    public void StartCoroutine(Runnable coroutine) {
        Thread thread = new Thread(coroutine);
        runningCoroutines.add(thread);
        thread.start();
    }

    public void StopCoroutine(Runnable coroutine) {
        for (Thread thread : runningCoroutines) {
            if (thread.getTarget() == coroutine) {
                thread.interrupt();
                runningCoroutines.remove(thread);
                return;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In the solution, the `CoroutineManager` class is implemented with a list to keep track of running coroutines. The `StartCoroutine` method creates a new thread for the given coroutine and adds it to the list. The `StopCoroutine` method iterates through the list to find the matching coroutine and interrupts its thread, effectively stopping its execution. The removed coroutine is then removed from the list. This design ensures that stopping a coroutine does not affect the execution of other coroutines.

