public boolean isMazeValid(int[][] matrix) {
    int m = matrix.length;
    int n = matrix[0].length;
    boolean[][] visited = new boolean[m][n];
    return isPathValid(matrix, 0, 0, visited, m, n);
}

private boolean isPathValid(int[][] matrix, int i, int j, boolean[][] visited, int m, int n) {
    if (i < 0 || i >= m || j < 0 || j >= n || matrix[i][j] == 1 || visited[i][j]) {
        return false;
    }
    if (i == m - 1 && j == n - 1) {
        return true;
    }
    visited[i][j] = true;
    if (isPathValid(matrix, i + 1, j, visited, m, n) || isPathValid(matrix, i, j + 1, visited, m, n) ||
        isPathValid(matrix, i - 1, j, visited, m, n) || isPathValid(matrix, i, j - 1, visited, m, n)) {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}
```

The `isMazeValid` function initializes a boolean array `visited` to keep track of visited cells and then calls the `isPathValid` function to check if there is a valid path from the top-left corner to the bottom-right corner. The `isPathValid` function uses backtracking to explore all possible paths while avoiding blocked cells and revisiting visited cells. If a valid path is found, it returns `true`; otherwise, it returns `false`.

