import itertools
import unittest

def permutation_decorator(test_class):
    class PermutationTestClass(unittest.TestCase):
        pass

    methods = [method for method in dir(test_class) if method.startswith('test_')]

    for permutation in itertools.permutations(methods):
        def generate_test(permutation):
            def test(self):
                for method_name in permutation:
                    getattr(test_class, method_name)(self)
            return test

        test_name = '_'.join(permutation)
        setattr(PermutationTestClass, f'test_{test_name}', generate_test(permutation))

    return PermutationTestClass
```
The `permutation_decorator` function takes the input test class and returns a new test class with all possible permutations of the original test class's methods as distinct test cases. It achieves this by using the `itertools.permutations` function to generate all possible permutations of the test class's methods and creating distinct test cases for each permutation in the new test class. Each permutation is a distinct test case with a unique name that reflects the permutation. The new test class can run all the permutations as individual test cases using the standard test runner.

