3. `register`: Takes a `productEntity` as a parameter and an optional `sku`. If `sku` is provided, it registers the product with the given `sku`. If `sku` is not provided, it generates a unique `sku` and registers the product. Returns a Promise that resolves to the registered product or an Error.
4. `removeProduct`: Takes a `sku` as a parameter and removes the product with the matching `sku`. Returns a Promise that resolves to the removed product or an Error if the product is not found.

Your task is to implement the `StoreProductManager` class and ensure that it adheres to the provided interface.

output:
```typescript
class StoreProductManager implements StoreProductInterface {
    private products: Product[] = [];

    async getAllProducts(): Promise<Product[]> {
        return this.products;
    }

    async getProductBySku(sku: string): Promise<Product | Error> {
        const product = this.products.find(p => p.sku === sku);
        if (product) {
            return product;
        } else {
            return new Error('Product not found');
        }
    }

    async register(productEntity: Product, sku?: string): Promise<Product | Error> {
        if (sku && this.products.some(p => p.sku === sku)) {
            return new Error('SKU already exists');
        }
        const newProduct: Product = {
            sku: sku || this.generateUniqueSku(),
            name: productEntity.name,
            price: productEntity.price,
            quantity: productEntity.quantity
        };
        this.products.push(newProduct);
        return newProduct;
    }

    async removeProduct(sku: string): Promise<Product | Error> {
        const index = this.products.findIndex(p => p.sku === sku);
        if (index !== -1) {
            const removedProduct = this.products.splice(index, 1)[0];
            return removedProduct;
        } else {
            return new Error('Product not found');
        }
    }

    private generateUniqueSku(): string {
        let sku = '';
        do {
            sku = Math.random().toString(36).substring(2, 10);
        } while (this.products.some(p => p.sku === sku));
        return sku;
    }
}
```

This solution provides a `StoreProductManager` class that implements the `StoreProductInterface` and manages products in memory. The class includes methods to get all products, get a product by SKU, register a new product, and remove a product. It also includes a method to generate a unique SKU for new products.

