public class CsvFormatterPlugin {
    public enum QuotePolicy {
        MINIMAL, ALL, NONE
    }

    public String setEscapeAndQuoteValue(String input, char delimiter, QuotePolicy policy, char escapeChar, char quoteChar, String additionalStr1, String additionalStr2) {
        if (input.isEmpty()) {
            return "";  // Return empty string for empty input
        }

        boolean needsQuotes = false;
        if (policy == QuotePolicy.ALL || (policy == QuotePolicy.MINIMAL && (input.contains(String.valueOf(delimiter)) || input.contains(String.valueOf(quoteChar))))) {
            needsQuotes = true;
        }

        StringBuilder formattedString = new StringBuilder();
        if (needsQuotes) {
            formattedString.append(quoteChar);  // Append opening quote
        }

        for (char c : input.toCharArray()) {
            if (c == quoteChar || c == escapeChar) {
                formattedString.append(escapeChar).append(c);  // Escape quote and escape characters
            } else {
                formattedString.append(c);
            }
        }

        if (needsQuotes) {
            formattedString.append(quoteChar);  // Append closing quote
        }

        return formattedString.toString();
    }
}
```

In the solution, the `setEscapeAndQuoteValue` method takes the input string, delimiter, quote policy, escape character, quote character, and two additional strings as parameters. It first checks if the input string is empty and returns an empty string if so. Then, it determines whether the input string needs quotes based on the quote policy and the presence of the delimiter or quote character. The method then iterates through the input string, escaping any quote or escape characters and appending the necessary quote characters based on the determined needs. Finally, it returns the formatted string.

