You are given a Java method that is intended to find the maximum value in an array of integers. However, the method contains a bug that prevents it from functioning correctly. Your task is to identify the bug and fix the method so that it correctly returns the maximum value in the array.

The original method is as follows:

```java
public class MaxValueFinder {
    public static int findMaxValue(int[] arr) {
        int part = arr[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < arr.length; i++) {
            if (arr[i] > part) {
                part = arr[i];
            }
        }
        return part;
    }
}
```

output:
The bug in the original method is that it initializes the `part` variable with the value at index 0 of the array, assuming that it is the maximum value. However, if the array contains only negative numbers, this assumption will be incorrect.

To fix this bug, we can initialize `part` with the minimum possible integer value (`Integer.MIN_VALUE`) instead of the value at index 0. This ensures that `part` starts with a value that is lower than any possible value in the array, allowing the loop to correctly find the maximum value.

Here's the corrected method:

```java
public class MaxValueFinder {
    public static int findMaxValue(int[] arr) {
        int part = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            if (arr[i] > part) {
                part = arr[i];
            }
        }
        return part;
    }
}
```

With this fix, the method will now correctly return the maximum value in the given array of integers.

