```csharp
public bool isHappyNumber(uint number)
{
    List<uint> sums = new List<uint>();
    uint sum = SumDigitsPowTwo(number);

    do
    {
        if (sum == 1)
        {
            return true;
        }

        sums.Add(sum);
        sum = SumDigitsPowTwo(sum);

    } while (!sums.Contains(sum));

    return false;
}

private uint SumDigitsPowTwo(uint num)
{
    uint sum = 0;
    while (num > 0)
    {
        uint digit = num % 10;
        sum += digit * digit;
        num /= 10;
    }
    return sum;
}
```

The `isHappyNumber` function first calculates the sum of the squares of the digits using the `SumDigitsPowTwo` function. It then iteratively calculates the sum of the squares of the digits of the result until either the sum becomes 1 (indicating a happy number) or a cycle is detected. If a cycle is detected, the function returns `false`. If the process ends with 1, the function returns `true`, indicating that the input number is a happy number.

