    let arg = ctx.get::<JsUnknown>(index)?;

    let result = match arg.get_type()? {
        ValueType::Undefined | ValueType::Null => None,
        ValueType::Number => {
            let number = arg.coerce_to_number()?;
            let uint_value = number.get_uint32().ok_or_else(|| {
                to_js_error("Failed to convert number to u16")
            })?;
            Some(uint_value as u16)
        }
        _ => {
            return Err(to_js_error("Expected number or undefined/null"));
        }
    };

    Ok(result)
}
```

In the solution, the `get_optional_u16` function is completed to handle different types of input arguments and return the appropriate `Result` containing an `Option` of a 16-bit unsigned integer (`u16`). The function uses the `quickjs_runtime` library to interact with JavaScript values and types. It checks the type of the input argument and coerces `Number` values to `u16`, returning `None` for `Undefined` or `Null` values, and raising an error for unexpected types. The function returns the result as a `Result` containing the computed `Option<u16>` or an error message.

