use std::num::ParseIntError;

fn parse_memory_limit(input: &str) -> Result<u32, ParseIntError> {
    match input.parse::<i32>() {
        Ok(num) if num > 0 => Ok(num as u32),
        _ => Err(ParseIntError { kind: std::num::IntErrorKind::PosOverflow }),
    }
}

fn parse_passes(input: &str) -> Result<Vec<String>, &'static str> {
    if input.is_empty() {
        return Ok(Vec::new());
    }

    let passes: Vec<&str> = input.split(',').collect();
    for pass in &passes {
        if pass.trim().is_empty() {
            return Err("Invalid pass name");
        }
    }

    Ok(passes.iter().map(|&x| x.to_string()).collect())
}
```

In the solution, the `parse_memory_limit` function uses the `parse` method to attempt to parse the input string as an integer. If successful and the parsed value is positive, it returns the parsed value as an unsigned integer. If the input is empty, contains non-numeric characters, or the parsed value is negative, it returns a `ParseIntError`.

The `parse_passes` function splits the input string by commas and validates each pass name. If the input is empty, it returns an empty vector. If any pass name is empty or contains invalid characters, it returns an error message. Otherwise, it returns a vector of parsed pass names.

